What:		/sys/devices/system/cpu/cpu[0-9]*/preferred
Date:		October 2026
Contact:	Linux kernel mailing list <linux-kernel@vger.kernel.org>
Description:
		Available with CONFIG_PREFERRED_CPU, including for
		CPUs that cannot be offlined. Reads return 1 if this CPU is
		preferred, or 0 otherwise.
		Root may write 1 to select the CPU or 0 to deselect it.
		Input is parsed with kstrtobool(), as for the online attribute;
		boolean spellings such as y/n and on/off are also accepted.

		Selecting an inactive CPU, deselecting the last preferred CPU,
		or writing an invalid boolean fails with EINVAL without changing
		the mask. Writing
		the current value is a no-op, except selecting an inactive CPU
		is always rejected.

		Writes across all CPUs are serialized with each other, the steal
		governor, and CPU hotplug. To replace a selection, select new CPUs
		before deselecting the old ones. The full mask can still be read from
		/sys/devices/system/cpu/preferred.

		CPU hotplug may subsequently change the mask: newly active CPUs
		become preferred, and deactivated CPUs cease to be preferred.
		Offlining the last preferred CPU makes all remaining active CPUs
		preferred and emits a notice in the kernel log.

		Manual control can be used together with STEAL_GOVERNOR. Manual writes
		change the current mask, not a persistent constraint on the
		governor: subsequent samples may change the selection again.
		Unloading the steal governor restores all active CPUs as
		preferred.
